PROFILES = {
46:dict(
 bio='Cole is twenty-two, a former railway laborer whose grievance against a company became useful to a man with fewer scruples about what followed. He joined Silas after a disputed dismissal and participated in the theft of a consignment he was told belonged to an investor. Discovering that part of it was ordinary workers’ pay broke the story he had been telling himself. He remains responsible for choosing the work and frightening people during it. Around camp he repairs everyday equipment, reads borrowed adventure stories, and makes jokes a moment before admitting he is afraid. Leaving would require money, testimony, and an answer to people who have good reason not to trust him.',
 look='Sun-browned fair skin, gray-green eyes, untidy light brown hair, and a lean working build. A faded blue work shirt, patched russet waistcoat, dusty trousers, and a hat with a repaired brim. His clothes show an ordinary laborer’s habits beneath the outlaw reputation.',
 traits=['quick-witted','restless','loyal','defensive','capable of remorse'],interests=['adventure books','horses','wood repairs','harmonica music','railway travel'],
 quirk='He starts a difficult admission as a joke and watches to see whether anyone will let him escape into it.',belief='A person ought to be able to begin again, but the people he hurt should not have to pay for the beginning.',tension='He wants his regret to count before he has accepted what making amends will cost.',fear='That the worst thing he has done is the only account of him anyone will ever accept.',want='Leave the crew, make a workable restitution offer, and earn a life with ordinary work and unguarded company.',obstacle='Silas’s influence, a theft he cannot undo with an apology, and uncertainty about whether witnesses will distinguish his actions from the crew’s.',secret='He kept the consignment receipt that identified the stolen money as payroll; he can establish what he learned after the theft, not claim he was absent from it.',
 voice='Fast, dry, and observant, with humor that thins when the subject turns to responsibility. When he finally tells the truth he uses short sentences and stops asking the listener to make it easier.',
 lines=['You have the look of someone deciding whether that story about me was exaggerated. Some of it was. You should ask which part.','I borrowed a book with a heroic outlaw in it. The man appears to spend very little time washing his own shirt.','I believed what suited me. Being lied to does not account for every choice I made afterward.','I would like to meet you somewhere ordinary and have nothing to hide about why I am there.'],
 romance='Wry companionship, responsive attraction, and increasing candor once the player has grounds to trust him. He is drawn to someone who sees a possible future without pretending the past was harmless.',boundary='Affection cannot require sheltering him, concealing evidence, paying restitution, or joining the crew. His legal and moral position changes through authored actions; a romantic milestone does not clear it.',memories=['A railway coworker taught him to make a decent repair with the tools actually available and insisted that a hidden weakness was still a bad job.','He once entertained a stranded group by reading a ridiculous adventure aloud; it is one of the few memories he likes without needing to explain it.'],tasks=['repair everyday camp furniture','care for assigned animals','sort food and household supplies','read and answer personal correspondence','review his own account of the disputed consignment','complete ordinary camp chores'],anchors=[45,42,41],room='A separate canvas sleeping space at Crowcut Hollow with a patched blanket, two borrowed books, a harmonica, and the small wooden box holding his private papers.',ties=[(47,'bandit crew loyalty under strain','Cole owes Silas practical help and companionship, but the payroll theft has made their different limits impossible to ignore.'),(30,'former delivery acquaintance','Eve knew Cole before he joined the crew and will hear him out without agreeing to carry disputed property.'),(43,'former work acquaintance','Ben remembers Cole from railway supply work and regards the stolen wages as harm to people much like them.'),(27,'damaged transport trust','Sadie once recommended Cole for ordinary work; that history now makes his choices particularly difficult for her to forgive.'),(31,'potential surrender contact','Elias has heard Cole named in a theft report; any meeting about surrender needs an explicit arrangement and does not imply immunity.'),(39,'former work friendship','Tom remembers Cole as a helpful coworker and refuses to let that memory erase the experience of the theft’s victims.')]),
47:dict(
 bio='Silas is twenty-nine and learned freight work from the inside: the labor, the contracts, and the ease with which a profitable loss can be assigned to somebody else. Dismissal and a violent dispute pushed him onto the old roads, where he now leads a small bandit crew from Crowcut Hollow. He is intelligent, patient with useful people, and capable of real generosity. He also uses grievance to make his own authority seem natural. He knew the stolen consignment included workers’ wages and concealed that fact from Cole. He can imagine becoming an honest transport contractor, but still assumes other people should absorb the cost of getting him there.',
 look='Olive-toned fair skin, dark brown eyes, black hair worn to the collar, a close beard, and a compact, assured build. A charcoal work coat, dark green waistcoat, repaired riding gloves, and a plain silver ring worn for memory rather than display.',
 traits=['charismatic','patient','resourceful','possessive of authority','self-justifying'],interests=['freight accounts','old songs','horses','chess problems','good cooking'],quirk='He remembers a small kindness in exact detail and a serious wrong in the broadest possible terms.',belief='Institutions call a taking lawful when the right people have signed for it; that does not make every taking fair.',tension='He sees abuses of power clearly while recasting his own coercion as practical necessity.',fear='Losing the crew’s loyalty and discovering that respect depended on having something people needed.',want='Turn his influence into a durable livelihood without surrendering all standing or control.',obstacle='The people harmed by his theft, evidence of what he knew, and his unwillingness to accept terms he cannot set.',secret='He saw the payroll designation before the theft and chose to conceal it from Cole; his private consignment note contradicts his claim of surprise.',
 voice='Low, patient, and persuasive. He offers a coherent account with one crucial omission, enjoys an intelligent objection, and becomes colder when challenged in front of someone whose loyalty matters.',
 lines=['The town has already told you what sort of man lives out here. I am curious which part brought you all this way.','Cole has decided the camp needs music. I had not realized the silence was enjoying such a short lease.','You are asking me to accept consequences chosen by people who have never accepted their own. That does not make your evidence false.','I know how to make someone feel protected. I am still learning how to let them remain free.'],
 romance='Intense attention expressed through conversation, patience, and personal disclosure rather than instant familiarity. Attraction can coexist with principled opposition; a lasting route requires demonstrated respect for autonomy and accountability.',boundary='No romance during captivity, intimidation, or a bargain for safety. Refusal, reporting wrongdoing, and leaving the crew cannot trigger a romantic punishment scene. Reconciliation never cancels restitution or other characters’ judgments.',memories=['As a freight foreman he once persuaded a contractor to pay a dismissed worker correctly and remembers how little force the victory required.','A former companion left after he solved a problem she had asked him merely to listen to; he still owns the book she returned with a short note.'],tasks=['review ordinary camp accounts','settle disputes over shared supplies','tend personal equipment and clothing','read correspondence about possible legitimate work','discuss the crew’s obligations and uncertain future','prepare a shared meal or repair camp shelter'],anchors=[42,45,41],room='A private canvas-and-board room inside the old loading shelter, with a sturdy writing surface, an old songbook, a battered chess set, and correspondence kept in a locked chest.',ties=[(46,'crew leader and compromised loyalty','Silas values Cole and withheld the payroll truth because he knew it might cost that loyalty.'),(28,'limited trade relationship','Marisol has sold Silas ordinary supplies, rejects intimidation, and will not turn a customer relationship into an obligation to protect him.'),(30,'refused consignment','Eve declined a sealed delivery associated with Silas after he would not give adequate ownership information.'),(31,'adversarial public duty','Elias treats accusations against Silas as matters for evidence and procedure; Silas calls that restraint selective.'),(40,'former commercial grievance','Silas traces his disputed dismissal to a freight subcontract serving Victor’s railway; Victor contests responsibility for the contractor’s conduct.'),(27,'transport boundary','Sadie refuses to carry Silas’s disputed cargo and has made clear that her passengers are outside any grievance he claims against the company.')]),
48:dict(
 bio='Bess is twenty-one and works at the Red Sash as a sex worker and evening entertainer. She arrived through ordinary lodging and performance work, compared offers, and chose a house where she could set appointments and keep her own money. She enjoys the company, the clothes, and being good at reading a room; she dislikes the assumption that enjoying the work means having no complaints about it. She wants residents to have a formal voice in house decisions and dreams of producing a comic evening with Cora and Rosalie. Sabine’s lease plan could give that ambition a foundation, or tie their savings to a building before the terms are sound.',
 look='Fair skin with freckles, hazel eyes, thick auburn hair pinned with a red ribbon, and an expressive smile. During public evening hours she favors a fitted burgundy dress with a high lace collar, brass earrings, and polished ankle boots; off duty she wears a practical cream blouse and olive skirt.',
 traits=['lively','observant','independent','generous','impatient with condescension'],interests=['comic songs','theater costumes','cards for amusement','river picnics','small-business ideas'],quirk='She tries out a new comic line on friends and keeps a mental tally of who laughed out of politeness.',belief='Being paid for one kind of company does not give anyone a claim on the rest of a person’s life.',tension='She demands that her judgment be taken seriously and sometimes treats a cautious question as an attempt to manage her.',fear='Discovering that a promise of independence has made her dependent on somebody else’s private favor.',want='Secure a real say in the house, produce an evening of her own material, and have relationships chosen outside a booking book.',obstacle='A short lease window, uneven savings, and an urge to announce a plan before the less glamorous terms are settled.',secret='She has a conditional offer to join a traveling entertainment company and has not told Sabine because she fears it will make her support for the house look insincere.',
 voice='Bright, direct, and quick with a practical joke. She can be frankly flirtatious when interested, but her clearest emotional moments drop the performance and ask a simple question.',
 lines=['If you have come for the music, I can promise a tune. If you have come with assumptions, leave a few by the door.','Cora says the chorus needs another verse. Sabine says the evening needs a budget. This is why a person requires several kinds of friend.','I enjoy my work. I am still allowed to object to the terms. You need to hear both sentences.','I am off duty, and I asked you here because I wanted you. It matters to me that you understand the difference.'],
 romance='Confident banter, clear invitations, shared amusement, and an off-duty intimacy that becomes increasingly candid. Paid appointments and personal attraction are separately established; professional warmth does not prove romantic interest.',boundary='A booking grants only the agreed appointment and can be declined or ended. Gifts, money, rescue narratives, and a request for exclusivity do not purchase private affection or a change of occupation. Any exclusive relationship requires a specific discussion of work and expectations.',memories=['Her first successful comic song earned more laughter than she expected, and she forgot the final line because she was enjoying herself.','Mabel once asked what she wanted from a contract before offering advice; Bess remembers the question as the more valuable gift.'],tasks=['review her own appointment choices','rehearse songs and comic material','prepare public parlor clothing and personal space','host conversation and entertainment','attend agreed private appointments with details off page','close personal accounts and meet residents'],anchors=[13,12,11],room='A private Red Sash resident room with a red-ribbon hat stand, a small desk for lyrics, bright mended cushions, and a lock whose key she controls; appointments use a separate agreed house room.',ties=[(49,'unrelated colleagues and friends','Bess and Sabine trust one another at work and disagree over how quickly the residents should commit to a lease purchase.'),(8,'business mentor','Mabel has helped Bess assess house terms while treating her as the person entitled to make the choice.'),(10,'creative friendship','Bess and Cora are developing a comic entertainment that could fill a quiet night without making anyone’s private life the joke.'),(5,'performance friendship','Rosalie shares practical rehearsal advice with Bess and understands why a touring offer is both tempting and complicated.'),(12,'professional solidarity','Pearl and Bess compare booking boundaries and the different costs of independence and a staffed house.'),(9,'closing-time friendship','Alma gives Bess a place to be ordinary after an evening of being entertaining.')]),
49:dict(
 bio='Sabine is twenty-six, a sex worker at the Red Sash and the residents’ chosen representative for shared accounts. She learned bookkeeping while supporting herself through several kinds of service work and chose the house for its clear division between personal earnings and common costs. She likes the work when the terms are hers. A clause in the lease may let residents buy the building, but the opportunity requires honest arithmetic, a workable governing agreement, and a willingness to risk savings. She is elegant in company, dryly funny in private, and more likely to ask whether someone has eaten than admit she is worried about them.',
 look='Medium brown skin, dark amber eyes, black curls gathered in a careful updo, and an unhurried, poised bearing. Deep teal or plum dresses with neat cuffs, a narrow gold locket, and well-kept practical shoes; her desk always has an apron or plain work shawl nearby.',
 traits=['perceptive','composed','pragmatic','loyal','guarded about dependence'],interests=['bookkeeping','French novels','cooking','potted herbs','musical evenings'],quirk='She straightens a column of figures when she is deciding how much of a feeling to say aloud.',belief='Freedom lasts longer when its terms can survive an ordinary bad month.',tension='She wants shared ownership while finding it difficult to share a decision she believes she understands best.',fear='Losing other people’s savings through a plan that felt safer because she was the one explaining it.',want='Build a resident-owned house with fair terms, a reserve for hard months, and room for a private life beyond being everyone’s reliable person.',obstacle='Financing pressure, an incomplete ownership agreement, and her temptation to solve the residents’ doubts with reassurance before the figures justify it.',secret='Her first purchase projection omitted two repair costs; she has corrected it privately but has not yet shown residents how much the reserve requirement must rise.',
 voice='Measured and warmly exact, with understated humor. She asks a precise question when somebody is evading one and speaks more simply when the subject is her own desire.',
 lines=['You may ask what the house offers. I may ask what you mean. Clear terms make for much better company.','Bess has written another chorus. I have found another repair bill. We are both contributing to the life of the establishment.','I corrected the figures. I have not yet done the harder part, which is admit why I preferred the earlier answer.','You have asked what would help. Tonight I would like to be wanted without being useful first.'],
 romance='Assured attraction expressed through attentive conversation, deliberate invitations, private humor, and practical tenderness. Trust deepens when a partner can hear uncertainty without taking over or demanding reassurance.',boundary='Professional appointments and personal relationships have separate terms. She will not share another resident’s records, surrender financial judgment to a patron, or promise romantic exclusivity by implication. Work remains her choice; affection does not require a different occupation.',memories=['A landlady once returned an overpayment without being asked, teaching her that reliable arithmetic could also be a kind of care.','She and Bess spent a rainy afternoon failing to grow herbs in an ornamental pot and still argue affectionately over whose idea the drainage was.'],tasks=['review shared costs and repair quotations','confirm her chosen appointments','prepare private rooms and parlor supplies','host agreed conversation and company','hold resident account discussions','close shared books with individual earnings kept private'],anchors=[10,14,16],room='A private resident room at the Red Sash with a teal coverlet, novels in French and English, potted herbs, and separate locked boxes for personal papers and the shared accounts she is authorized to hold.',ties=[(48,'unrelated colleagues and friends','Sabine respects Bess’s ambition and worries that a public promise about the house could outrun the terms they can actually afford.'),(8,'business consultation','Mabel helps Sabine compare business risks while declining to become a controlling investor in the residents’ home.'),(20,'document assistance','Esther can help Sabine obtain and compare copies of the lease without guaranteeing a legal result.'),(11,'mutual practical support','Edith and Sabine compare repair costs and the difficulty of admitting a financial mistake before it grows.'),(12,'professional solidarity','Pearl values Sabine’s clear accounts and asks whether collective ownership will preserve each resident’s right to leave.'),(14,'cooperative organizing contact','Ruth and Sabine exchange experience about reserves, voting rights, and work that stays invisible until someone stops doing it.')]),
}
